  "overview": {
    "text": "An inverse is a mathematical partner that reverses an operation or transformation: applying an operation and then its inverse returns the starting value. For functions, if f(a)=b, then f⁻¹(b)=a; composing the two in either order gives the identity, where defined. A function has an inverse function only when it is one-to-one, meaning different inputs have different outputs; restricting its domain can sometimes make this possible. For a square matrix A, its inverse A⁻¹ satisfies AA⁻¹=A⁻¹A=I, with I the identity matrix. A matrix inverse exists exactly when its determinant is nonzero. Function notation f⁻¹ means “inverse function,” not 1/f.",
    "source_urls": [
      "https://openstax.org/books/college-algebra/pages/3-7-inverse-functions?modal=MH",
      "https://openstax.org/books/precalculus-2e/pages/9-7-solving-systems-with-inverses",
      "https://math.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Linear_Algebra/Linear_Algebra_with_Applications_(Nicholson)/03:_Determinants_and_Diagonalization/3.02:_Determinants_and_Matrix_Inverses"
    ]
  },
  "key_facts": [
    {
      "text": "For an invertible function, input-output pairs switch: if f(a)=b, then f⁻¹(b)=a. The compositions f⁻¹∘f and f∘f⁻¹ return the original input where defined.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://openstax.org/books/college-algebra/pages/3-7-inverse-functions?modal=MH"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "A function must be one-to-one to have an inverse function; on a graph, this is tested with the horizontal line test.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://openstax.org/books/college-algebra/pages/3-7-inverse-functions?modal=MH"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "The full function f(x)=x² is not one-to-one, but restricting its domain to x≥0 gives it the inverse f⁻¹(x)=√x.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://openstax.org/books/college-algebra/pages/3-7-inverse-functions?modal=MH"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "A matrix inverse is defined for a square matrix A by the two checks AA⁻¹=I and A⁻¹A=I; I has ones on its main diagonal and zeros elsewhere.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://openstax.org/books/precalculus-2e/pages/9-7-solving-systems-with-inverses"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "A square matrix has an inverse exactly when its determinant is not zero; if the determinant is zero, it has no inverse.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://math.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Linear_Algebra/Linear_Algebra_with_Applications_(Nicholson)/03:_Determinants_and_Diagonalization/3.02:_Determinants_and_Matrix_Inverses"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "For a nonzero number a, its multiplicative inverse is its reciprocal, 1/a; zero has no multiplicative inverse.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://openstax.org/books/precalculus-2e/pages/9-7-solving-systems-with-inverses"
      ]
    }
  ],
